export anchor PluginCapabilities:
    description:
        A plugin is code from somewhere else running on the user's machine, so
        it may do only what its declaration lists. Reading the compiled
        project is always allowed, since it changes nothing. Everything else
        is a capability, and anything not listed is refused.

        ```piton
        \\\
        capabilities:
            process: [claude, codex]
            files: read
            temporaryFiles: true
            outputs: [.claude/**, **/CLAUDE.md]
            interactive: true
            edits: [piton.config.pi]
        \\\
        ```

    process:
        The programs the plugin may run, by name, looked up on PATH. Only
        these can be started, and only these can be looked for on PATH. A
        program is named as it is run, so `claude` allows `claude` and
        nothing else.

    files:
        read lets the plugin read files inside the project root, and list the
        files in a directory there; none, the default, doesn't. A path that
        leads outside the project is refused either way. A plugin never writes
        files in the project itself: its build returns a plan, which the host
        writes within its outputs.

    outputs:
        The paths in the project a plugin's build may write, as glob
        patterns relative to the project root: `*` matches within one
        directory and `**` across directories. They are written without
        quotes, which Piton would keep as part of the text. A planned file or
        copy outside them is an error, and nothing is written. A plugin with a build and no
        outputs can only report diagnostics.

    edits:
        The files in the project a plugin may propose to change, as glob
        patterns relative to the project root, like outputs. A proposed edit
        is only ever applied when the user agrees, or asks for it by running
        the plugin's command; a plugin never writes a source file itself. An
        edit outside them is an error, and nothing of it is offered.

    interactive:
        true lets the plugin ask the user to choose from a menu on the
        terminal. The menu only ever shows when stdin and stdout are
        terminals; otherwise nothing is asked and nothing is chosen, so a
        plugin never waits on someone who isn't there.

    temporaryFiles:
        true lets the plugin write temporary files, outside the project, each
        removed when its command ends, like the file OpenCode reads its
        instructions from.

    refusal:
        A refused operation throws in the plugin, saying what it tried and
        which capability would allow it, like "plugin `hello` may not run
        `ls`: its declaration does not list it under capabilities.process".
        The plugin can catch it; uncaught, the command fails with it.

    unknown:
        A capability that isn't one of these makes the declaration invalid,
        so a plugin never runs believing it has something it doesn't.

    visibility:
        `piton plugins` shows what each plugin may do, so what a project's
        plugins can reach is one command away.

    whatItIsNot:
        Capabilities limit what the plugin's own code does. A program it is
        allowed to run is not sandboxed by them: an agentic tool started by
        the test plugin can do what that tool allows, which is why the test
        plugin starts each tool in its own read-only mode where it has one.
